e second set  rejection which is quicker  and it only takes 10 days to reject the  second set of craft  like you have rejection of one graph and  you again grafted  this in the same location how fast that  graph would be rejected is shown  here now let's talk about what are the  key cell types  that are behind graph rejection  and the answer comes from classical  experiments  here is a strain of mouse which is known  as a naive strain  right so first screen graft would be  given to this particular  strain b from a mouse strain a  so the graft is introduced to mouse  strain b  so there would be first set of rejection  possibly right  if the graft is doesn't graft is not  matching and the immune system of this  mouse is considering it to be  alien species or a kind of like a  foreign substance right  and that happens in a course of 14 days  as we have learned  then there could be a second set of  grafting and that would be rejected  in a time span of a further 10 days  right now if  particular cell types such as t cells  are taken from a first set rejection  mice and injected to a naive strain  then it shows a kinetics which is very  similar to a second set rejection  right so after putting the prime  t cells in the strain b  mouse and then grafting the tissue leads  to a second set rejection  the kinetics became faster that tells us  in turn t cells or t helper cells  might have a role in terms of craft  rejection  there is another set of experiment which  tells this phenomena  in a more confirmative way so there are  four set of mouse  and we have given graphs now along with  graphs  in set two three and four we have given  specific antibodies such as antiseriate  antibodies to neutralize the cd8  t cell population then in the set 3 we  have given anti cd4  and anti cd4 to neutralize the  t helper cells and lastly we give a  combination of anti-cd4 and ndcda  and let's look at the survival of the  graft in each of these cases  we see this kind of graph when we have 